    "title": "US University Bans Traditional Somali Attire on Campus",
    "original_title": "Jaamacad Maraykan Ah Oo Mamnuucday Durag, Bonnado Iyo Kabaha somalida",
    "slug": "us-university-bans-traditional-somali-attire-on-campus",
    "summary": "Tuskegee University in Alabama has implemented a new dress code prohibiting students from wearing traditional Somali items like durags, bonnado, and flat shoes in classrooms and dining halls, sparking debate within the Somali diaspora.",
    "content": "Tuskegee University, a historically Black institution in Alabama, has introduced a revised dress code that restricts certain types of attire on campus. The policy, which took effect this academic year, forbids students from wearing durags, bonnado (a type of headwrap), and flat-soled shoes such as sandals in academic buildings and dining facilities. University administrators say the measure aims to promote a professional environment and prepare students for post-graduation workplace expectations, extending similar restrictions to formal events.\n\nThe decision has drawn mixed reactions, particularly from Somali students and diaspora communities who view these items as culturally significant. Durags, often worn for hair maintenance, and traditional wraparound garments hold practical and cultural value for many Black and Muslim students. Critics argue that the policy disproportionately affects students from these backgrounds, while supporters contend that dress codes are common at many universities and can help instill discipline.\n\nTuskegee's move is not isolated; several US colleges have revisited dress code policies in recent years, balancing cultural expression with institutional norms. For Somali students, many of whom navigate multiple cultural identities, such rules can feel particularly intrusive. The debate underscores broader conversations about inclusivity and accommodation in higher education, especially for diaspora communities with distinct traditions.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this issue resonates deeply, as many families value education as a path to success yet also cherish their heritage. Students at Tuskegee and elsewhere may now face a choice between adhering to campus policies and expressing their cultural identity. The university has not indicated plans to revise the policy but has invited student feedback, suggesting that discourse may continue.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Dalsan here: https://radiodalsan.com/2026/08/22/jaamacad-maraykan-ah-oo-mamnuucday-durag-bonnado-iyo-kabaha-somalida/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=jaamacad-maraykan-ah-oo-mamnuucday-durag-bonnado-iyo-kabaha-somalida",
